According to its graph, the one-sided limits at $x=-2$ are different for function $g$ and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2}g(x)$ doesn't exist.

However, before we jump to any conclusions regarding $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2}\Big(g(x)h(x)\Big)$, let's consider the one-sided limits:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2^-}g(x)=4}
\\\\
&\purpleD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2^+}g(x)=2}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2^-}h(x)=\lim_{x\to -2^+}h(x)=2}
\end{align}$

We can find each one-sided limit of $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2}\Big(g(x)h(x)\Big)$ using the fact that the limit of a product of functions is the product of the individual limits:

$\begin{align}
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2^-}\Big(g(x)h(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to -2^-}g(x)\cdot \lim_{x\to -2^-}h(x)
\\\\
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2^+}\Big(g(x)h(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to -2^+}g(x)\cdot \lim_{x\to -2^+}h(x)
\end{align}$
